"L before and after",
"Friction between the faces pushes the two disks with equal and opposite torques, so whatever angular momentum one loses the other gains: L = I₁ω₁ + I₂ω₂ stays the same. Kinetic energy does not: the faces rub while they slip, and the heat is I₁I₂(ω₁ − ω₂)²/{#}(I₁ + I₂), however large or small the friction. Friction only sets how long it takes: Δt = I₁I₂|ω₁ − ω₂|/((I₁ + I₂)τ).",
"Model: uniform disks on one axis, the bottom one on a frictionless bearing. The top disk is set down without a bounce and presses with its weight spread evenly over its face (R₂ ≤ R₁), so kinetic friction gives τ = ⅔μkM₂gR₂ while they slip. Air drag neglected.",
